 "title": "15-Minute Thinking Exercise for Any Topic",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator demonstrates a 15-minute thinking exercise to break down any random topic without using AI or Google, using the sentence 'Big ideas contain good and bad debates' as a structured prompt framework. Each letter in the sentence's words guides a different angle of analysis, such as big picture, importance, components, pros, cons, and current debates.",
 "key_points": [
  "Start with the sentence: 'Big Ideas Contain Good & Bad Debates'. The first letter of each word gives you a different prompt to think about.",
  "B - Big Idea / Background: Think about the big picture and context of the topic (e.g., world's largest youth population navigating rapid modern change).",
  "I - Ideas / Importance: Why does this idea matter today? (e.g., youth drive economic growth and cultural identity).",
  "C - Contain / Content / Components: What are the components of the topic? (e.g., college students, corporate professionals, gig workers, rural entrepreneurs).",
  "G - Good / Good things / Opportunities: What are the positive aspects? (e.g., ambitious, love hustle culture and startups, most digitally literate generation).",
  "B - Bad / Bad things / Problems / Risks: What are the problems faced? (e.g., fierce exam competition, job anxiety, intense lifestyle comparison).",
  "D - Debates / Current affairs / Discussions: What are the ongoing discussions? (e.g., paper leaks, student protests, Gen Z work culture, reservation versus merit)."
 ],
 "content": "Here's how I turn any random topic into a 15-minute thinking exercise without using AI or Google. Let's say your topic is Indian youth. You just need to remember one sentence: Big ideas contain good and bad debates. And the first letter of each word in the sentence will give you a different prompt to think about. Big is for the big picture, the background. What is it? What's the context right now about Indian youth? You might think about the world's largest youth population who's navigating rapid modern change. Second is the idea, the importance of this idea. Why does this matter today? They are the primary engine that will drive economic growth and cultural identity for our country. The third is the contents, the content, the components of this particular topic. What do they comprise of? College students, corporate professionals, gig workers, rural entrepreneurs. Fourth is the good things about Indian youth. What are the opportunities? Um they are highly ambitious, they love hustle culture, they love startups, and they are the most digitally literate generation of of the country. Then we come to the bad things. What are the problems that they are facing? Um fierce exam competition, anxiety, job anxiety, um intense lifestyle comparison. Finally, we come to the debates. Any ongoing discussion that is going on about this particular topic. Paper leaks, student protest, Gen Z work culture, reservation versus merit. Just a single sentence that will help you have a complete structured brain dump of any topic in minutes. Uh this sentence helps me break down any topic that I might need to be able to talk about. Instagram reel/post by Swati’s Notes. Text / caption: To tackle brainrot 🫣 #Creativity #writercommunity #writersofinstagram #think #brainrot Text on screen: 15 Minute Thinking Exercise, Big Ideas Contain Good & Bad Debates, Big - Big Idea/Background, Ideas - Importance of this idea, Contain - Content/Components, Good - good things/opportunities, Bad - bad things/problems/risks, Debates - current affairs/discussions",
